    "intro": "A diagonal splits any quadrilateral into two triangles. Because each triangle has an angle sum of $180^{\\circ}$, the four interior angles of a quadrilateral add to $360^{\\circ}$.",
    "facts": [
      "The interior angle sum of a quadrilateral is $360^{\\circ}$: $a+b+c+d=360^{\\circ}$.",
      "**Proof.** The diagonal $BD$ splits quadrilateral $ABCD$ into $\\triangle ABD$ and $\\triangle BCD$. Each has an angle sum of $180^{\\circ}$, and together their six angles make up the four angles of $ABCD$, so the sum is $2\\times180^{\\circ}=360^{\\circ}$.",
      "The result also holds for a non-convex quadrilateral, where one interior angle is reflex (more than $180^{\\circ}$).",
      "If the three known angles total less than $180^{\\circ}$, the fourth interior angle must be reflex.",
      "When angles are given as expressions, add the expressions, set the total equal to $360$ and solve."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Write an equation using the angle sum.",
      "Simplify the equation.",
      "Solve for the unknown."
    ]
  },
